---
import DocPage from "../../layouts/DocPage.astro";
import IndexList from "../../components/reference/IndexList.astro";
import Heading from "../../components/reference/Heading.astro";
import JumpBar from "../../components/reference/JumpBar.astro";
import { codeTheme } from "../../code-theme.mjs";
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import { api, prose } from "../../reference";
import { link } from "../../site";

const request = `POST /agent.v1.AgentService/GetAgent
Content-Type: application/json
X-Komrad-Agent-Id: 019991a5-3c4e-7b12-8f3a-1c2d3e4f5a6b
X-Komrad-Session-Id: 3b438df1-9e00-4716-a74c-e1ed2292e091

{ "id": "019991a5-3c4e-7b12-8f3a-1c2d3e4f5a6b" }`;

const record = `{
  "id": "019991a5-3c4e-7b12-8f3a-1c2d3e4f5a6b",
  "name": "update-docs-language",
  "created_at": "2026-09-26T22:56:41.128390Z",
  "state_changed_at": "2026-09-26T23:14:02.551007Z"
}`;

const page = `{ "pagination": { "limit": 50, "cursor": "eyJpIjoiMDE5OTkxYTUtM2M0ZS03YjEyLThmM2EtMWMyZDNlNGY1YTZiIn0" } }`;

const pageResponse = `{
  "agents": [ ... ],
  "next_page_token": "eyJpIjoiMDE5OTkxYTUtM2M0ZS03YjEyLThmM2EtMWMyZDNlNGY1YTZiIn0"
}`;

const failure = `HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found
Content-Type: application/json
X-Request-Id: 019991a6-8b0e-7c3d-9e1f-2a3b4c5d6e7f

{ "code": "not_found", "message": "agent 019991a5-3c4e-7b12-8f3a-1c2d3e4f5a6b not found" }`;
---

<DocPage
  title="API"
  description="Every endpoint the service answers, and how to reach it over Connect, from the CLI, and as an MCP tool."
>
  <JumpBar
    items={[
      { href: "#requests", label: "Requests" },
      { href: "#responses", label: "Responses" },
      { href: "#pagination", label: "Pagination" },
      { href: "#errors", label: "Errors" },
      ...api.services.map((service) => ({ href: `#${service.slug}`, label: service.title })),
    ]}
  />

  <section class="convention">
    <Heading id="requests">Requests</Heading>
    <p>
      The service listens on one loopback address, and every endpoint on it is a Connect RPC. Calling one is a JSON
      <code>POST</code> to the path on its method page. This is the call for one agent by its id:
    </p>
    <Code code={request} lang="http" theme={codeTheme} />
    <p>
      The two <code>X-Komrad</code> headers are how an agent says who it is. The CLI and the MCP server add them from
      the agent's environment, so an agent never sets them by hand, and a method that limits what an agent may do, such
      as archiving only its own children, decides on them. A call without them comes from a person, and a person may do
      anything.
    </p>
    <p>
      The CLI command and the MCP tool make exactly this request, which is why each method page shows the same call
      three ways. The service answers this machine only: a request carrying an <code>Origin</code> header from anywhere
      else is refused.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="convention">
    <Heading id="responses">Responses</Heading>
    <p>
      What comes back is JSON following the protobuf JSON mapping, with every field spelled exactly as the tables on the
      method pages spell it. The agent asked for above comes back like this:
    </p>
    <Code code={record} lang="json" theme={codeTheme} />
    <p>
      Every id is a UUID, and it is the one handle a record keeps for life, archived or not, which is why calls take ids
      rather than names. A name is a label for people: an agent may be renamed, and two workflows may each hold an agent
      of the same name, so a method that accepts one says what it does when the name is ambiguous. Every timestamp is
      RFC 3339 in UTC.
    </p>
    <p>
      A field the method page marks optional is left out when it has no value, never sent as null, so a merged pull
      request carries a <code>merged_at</code> and an open one does not. That strictness runs both ways: a request
      naming a field the method does not have is refused rather than having the field quietly dropped.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="convention">
    <Heading id="pagination">Pagination</Heading>
    <p>
      A list that can grow without bound comes back a page at a time. Ask for a page with a <code>pagination</code>
      object holding a limit, which is 20 when left out and never more than 100, and a cursor, which is empty for the
      first page:
    </p>
    <Code code={page} lang="json" theme={codeTheme} />
    <p>
      While there is more to read, the page comes back with a <code>next_page_token</code>. Hand it back as the cursor,
      exactly as it came, and the next page follows on from where this one ended. A page without a token is the last
      one. Each list says on its own page what order it comes back in.
    </p>
    <Code code={pageResponse} lang="json" theme={codeTheme} />
  </section>

  <section class="convention">
    <Heading id="errors">Errors</Heading>
    <p>
      When a call cannot be answered, the response is a Connect error: a code saying what kind of thing went wrong, and
      a message saying what, for this request. The same call as above, with an id that names nothing:
    </p>
    <Code code={failure} lang="http" theme={codeTheme} />
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Code</th>
          <th>Meaning</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <code>invalid_argument</code>
          </td>
          <td>The request broke a rule on the method page, or named something in the wrong form.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <code>not_found</code>
          </td>
          <td>An id named nothing.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <code>already_exists</code>
          </td>
          <td>What the call would create is already there, and the message names it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <code>failed_precondition</code>
          </td>
          <td>The record exists but is not in a state the call can act on, such as an agent mid-turn.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <code>permission_denied</code>
          </td>
          <td>The caller may not act on that record, such as a child mailing outside its family.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <code>resource_exhausted</code>
          </td>
          <td>A limit was reached, such as the number of agents. Trying again will not help.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <code>internal</code>
          </td>
          <td>The service failed rather than the request.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The <code>X-Request-Id</code> header is on every response, not only failures. It is the trace id the service
      logged the call under, and <a href={link("/api/log/get-log-lines")}>get_log_lines</a> takes it to show what the
      service did with the call.
    </p>
    <p>
      A method that takes a list of ids, such as <a href={link("/api/agent/archive-agents")}>archive_agents</a>, treats
      it as one request. If any id is unknown, or any one of them is refused, the call fails naming why and none of them
      change.
    </p>
  </section>

  {api.services.map((service) => (
    <section class="service">
      <Heading id={service.slug}>
        <a href={link(`/api/${service.slug}`)}>{service.title}</a>
      </Heading>
      <div class="service-description" set:html={prose(service.description)} />
      <IndexList
        rows
        items={service.methods.map((method) => ({
          href: link(`/api/${service.slug}/${method.slug}`),
          title: method.tool,
          summary: method.summary,
          id: `${service.slug}-${method.slug}`,
        }))}
      />
    </section>
  ))}
</DocPage>
